---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const capmCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'beta', label: 'asset beta (market sensitivity)', min: 0, max: 2, step: 0.05, value: 1.2 },
    { name: 'rf', label: 'risk-free rate', min: 0, max: 6, step: 0.25, value: 3, unit: '%' },
    { name: 'mrp', label: 'market risk premium', min: 2, max: 8, step: 0.5, value: 5, unit: '%' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'CAPM expected return = rf + β·MRP', expr: 'rf + beta*mrp' },
    { label: 'extra return demanded for this beta', expr: 'beta*mrp' },
    { label: 'zero-beta asset return', expr: 'rf' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/finance/expert/asset-pricing-models">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the pricing summit of the finance ladder, and it stands on lessons you have
    already climbed. <em>Stocks and Bonds</em> introduced the assets being priced;
    <em>Diversification and Portfolios</em> gave you the first taste of combining them;
    <em>Time Value of Money (NPV)</em> supplied the discounting machinery that turns a required
    return into a value; and the advanced lesson <em>Portfolio Theory (Markowitz)</em> built
    the efficient frontier this lesson prices against. Asset pricing is the third step of that
    ladder: given a portfolio theory, what return does any single asset deserve?
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>choose between arithmetic and geometric means, and say why volatile assets compound worse than their averages suggest;</li>
    <li>compute beta from covariances and distinguish it from total volatility;</li>
    <li>use the Sharpe ratio to compare strategies, and name the losses variance hides;</li>
    <li>derive and apply CAPM — expected return = risk-free rate + beta × market premium — as a live calculator;</li>
    <li>read the empirical record (the flat security market line, the factor zoo) and say what CAPM is still trusted to do.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Asset pricing asks <strong>what return an investment should offer, given its risk</strong> —
    and the discipline's answer changed how every pension, endowment and company is managed.
    The unit's three topics are the field in miniature: measure return and risk; combine assets
    into portfolios; then price any single asset off the portfolio. CAPM (Sharpe, Lintner and
    Mossin, 1964–66; Nobel 1990) is the hinge: only <em>market</em> risk is paid, because
    diversifiable risk can be removed for free — so it earns nothing. The model is elegant,
    empirically imperfect, and still the backbone of every discount-rate debate.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the CAPM equation as a live calculator — beta is the whole story" widget="sliderLab" config={capmCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> three sliders — asset beta, the risk-free rate, the market
    risk premium — drive three live readouts. Slide beta first and watch the expected return
    move linearly: at beta 0 the asset earns only the risk-free rate, at beta 1 the market's
    own return. Slide the risk-free rate and the whole schedule shifts; slide the premium and
    it steepens. The middle readout — the extra return demanded for this beta — is the part
    diversifiable risk never earns, which is the model's entire argument compressed into one
    number. Keep it in view when the sections below dissect what beta does and does not
    measure.
  </p>

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> asset pricing asks what return an investment should offer given
  its risk, and CAPM answers: only market risk is paid. Before pricing anything, we need the
  raw material — how return and risk are actually measured.</p>

  <h2>Return and risk measurement: the raw material</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Returns.</strong> An asset's return is its price change plus income, and the first
    discipline is choosing the average. Arithmetic mean (the simple average of periodic returns)
    is the right input for <em>expected</em> future returns; the geometric mean (compounded:
    −50% followed by +60% leaves you at 0.8, a −20% total, not +5%) is the right description of
    <em>realised</em> growth, and it is always lower — the gap grows with volatility, which is
    why volatile assets compound worse than their arithmetic averages suggest. US large-cap
    equities returned roughly 10% nominal per year over the last century (geometric, about
    6–7% above inflation); Treasury bills near 3%.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Risk.</strong> The standard measure is variance (σ²) — the average squared
    deviation from the mean — or its square root, the standard deviation. US equities run
    σ ≈ 15–20% a year, government bonds nearer 5–8%, cash near 1%. Risk also wears a directional
    face: beta, an asset's sensitivity to the market, computed as β = Cov(rᵢ, rₘ) / Var(rₘ).
    Note what beta is <em>not</em>: not the asset's total volatility, but the market-linked
    slice of it — a low-volatility asset can carry high beta, and vice versa.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The ratio.</strong> The Sharpe ratio — (Rₚ − R_f) / σₚ, excess return per unit of
    total risk — is the single most used comparison metric. A fund returning 10% with 15%
    volatility in a 3% rate world scores (10 − 3)/15 ≈ 0.47; its rival at 8% with 9% volatility
    scores 0.56 and wins. The measure's known weaknesses: it punishes upside volatility
    symmetrically with downside, and it collapses when returns are not bell-shaped — which is
    why practitioners add drawdown and skew measures. But as a common currency for "return per
    unit of risk", it organises everything that follows.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Counting losses: the measures variance hides.</strong> Standard deviation treats
    +20% and −20% identically, but investors do not. The practitioner's additions: downside
    deviation (only returns below target enter the denominator — the Sortino ratio swaps it
    into Sharpe's formula), maximum drawdown (worst peak-to-trough loss — the measure that
    decides whether a strategy survives its investors' nerves), and Value-at-Risk (the loss
    threshold not exceeded, say, 95% of the time — with its notorious blindness to exactly
    the 5% tail it excludes). The lesson generalises: risk is multidimensional, and any
    single-number summary embeds a claim about which losses matter. Portfolio policy written
    on variance alone fails in exactly the ways (leverage, illiquidity, skew) that produce
    the famous blow-ups.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "higher risk investments pay higher returns, so risk
    generates reward." Wrong direction: risk does not <em>produce</em> returns; expected return
    is the <em>compensation</em> demanded for bearing risk that cannot be diversified away. Most
    of any single stock's risk is idiosyncratic — and the pricing models pay approximately
    nothing for it, because you could have diversified it away at no cost.
  </p>
  <p><strong>So far:</strong> the measurement toolkit is settled — arithmetic versus geometric
  means, variance and beta, the Sharpe ratio and the losses it hides. Next comes the step that
  makes the pricing possible: combining assets so that some of that risk simply vanishes.</p>

  <h2>Portfolio planning and construction: Markowitz's insight</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The mechanism.</strong> Markowitz (1952, Journal of Finance; Nobel 1990) converted
    diversification from folk wisdom into arithmetic. For two assets, portfolio variance is
    σₚ² = w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2·w₁·w₂·ρ·σ₁·σ₂ — the correlation ρ is the engine. When assets are
    imperfectly correlated, the portfolio's risk falls <em>below</em> the weighted average of
    its parts: you get the weighted-average return with less-than-weighted-average risk.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Worked example.</strong> Take a 60/40 stocks-and-bonds mix with σ(stocks) = 16%,
    σ(bonds) = 6%, ρ = 0.2. The weighted-average risk would be 0.6 × 16 + 0.4 × 6 = 12%. The
    true portfolio risk: √(0.36 × 0.0256 + 0.16 × 0.0036 + 2 × 0.6 × 0.4 × 0.2 × 0.016 × 0.06)
    = √0.01071 ≈ 10.4%. Diversification delivered 1.6 points of "free" risk reduction — before
    any skill. Repeating this across a correlation matrix and sweeping the weights traces the
    <em>efficient frontier</em>: the best return available at each risk level, which is what
    "asset allocation" means operationally.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The limits, honestly stated.</strong> Correlations are not constants: they rise in
    crises (2022's simultaneous stock-bond slide being the fresh counterexample to the classic
    negative correlation), so frontier-based risk budgets understate tail risk exactly when it
    matters. Inputs are unobservable (expected returns must be estimated — the estimation-error
    problem that makes optimisers "error maximisers"). And the model measures diversifiable
    variance only; it is silent about illiquidity, leverage and ruin. Professional construction
    therefore wraps Markowitz in constraints, shrinkage estimation and scenario overlays — the
    arithmetic survives as the skeleton, not the whole body.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Implementation: rebalancing as the quiet return source.</strong> A portfolio left
    alone drifts: the winners grow past their weights, and the risk budget rides with them.
    Rebalancing back to targets (calendar-based or band-based) restores the design — and,
    because it systematically sells relatively expensive assets and buys relatively cheap
    ones, it earns a small "diversification return" in choppy markets (the arithmetic of
    constant-mix strategies: with any spread between asset returns that later mean-reverts,
    the rebalanced portfolio compounds more than the buy-and-hold). The costs are real too —
    taxes, trading, tracking error regret — which is why the professional compromise is
    bands (rebalance only when weights drift past thresholds) rather than calendars. The
    pension version is the glide path: target-date funds that start equity-heavy and de-risk
    toward retirement, automation applied to Markowitz.
  </p>
  <p><strong>So far:</strong> Markowitz turned diversification into arithmetic — imperfect
  correlations buy less-than-weighted-average risk — and rebalancing keeps the design from
  drifting. With the frontier built, the pricing question finally has a foothold: what should
  a single asset earn, given what the portfolio can already do for free?</p>

  <h2>CAPM: pricing the one risk that pays</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The model.</strong> Add a risk-free asset and one assumption that does the heavy
    lifting — every investor holds the mean-variance optimal portfolio — and the market
    portfolio becomes the efficient frontier's tangency point. The price of any asset follows:
    E(Rᵢ) = R_f + βᵢ × (E(Rₘ) − R_f). Only beta enters: expected return is the risk-free rate
    plus beta times the market risk premium. The widget above is the equation live — set β = 1.2,
    risk-free 4%, premium 5%, and the required return is 4 + 1.2 × 5 = 10%. A zero-beta asset
    earns the risk-free rate; negative-beta assets (portfolio insurance) earn <em>less</em>,
    because they hedge the market.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What it is used for.</strong> Despite its empirical troubles, CAPM survives because
    practice needs a number: cost of equity in valuation (the discount rate in every DCF),
    hurdle rates for corporate projects, performance attribution (alpha = realised return minus
    the CAPM-predicted return). It is the default because it is simple, transparent and hard to
    beat systematically — a rough tool that is still sharper than its alternatives' complexity.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The empirical record.</strong> The security market line comes out flatter than the
    model says: low-beta stocks earn more than predicted, high-beta less (Black, Jensen and
    Scholes, 1972; the "betting against beta" strategy of Frazzini and Pedersen, 2014,
    monetises it). Fama–French (1993) added size (small beats large) and value (cheap beats
    expensive) factors — Nobel 2013 — and momentum (Jegadeesh and Titman, 1993) joined the
    standard toolkit. Then the "factor zoo" (Cochrane's term): hundreds of claimed factors,
    many of them statistical flukes of multiple testing — Harvey, Liu and Zhu (2016)
    recommended a t-statistic bar of 3.0+ (against the conventional 2.0) to clear the prior
    probability that most published anomalies are noise. The honest summary: expected returns
    are multidimensional; beta is the first dimension, not the only one.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "beta is a stock's volatility." Beta is covariance with the
    market divided by market variance — the co-movement component only. A utility stock with
    12% total volatility and little market sensitivity has a beta well under 1; a leveraged
    tech stock can have both high volatility and high beta. Confusing the two misprices
    everything downstream, starting with the discount rate.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Beta, computed once by hand.</strong> Take a stock with 30% volatility whose
    returns correlate 0.7 with a market running 15% volatility. Beta = ρ × (σᵢ / σₘ) =
    0.7 × (30/15) = 1.4: the stock amplifies market moves by 40%. At a 4% risk-free rate and
    5% premium, CAPM demands 4 + 1.4 × 5 = 11%. Portfolios aggregate linearly: a fund that is
    60% of that stock and 40% of a β = 0.6 defensive share has beta 0.6 × 1.4 + 0.4 × 0.6 =
    1.08 and required return 9.4%. Notice what did not matter: the stock's total 30% risk —
    only its market-linked slice was priced. That asymmetry is CAPM's whole content, and the
    widget above lets you watch the same computation live.
  </p>

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "diversification eliminates risk." It eliminates
  <em>idiosyncratic</em> risk — the company-specific noise that averages out across
  holdings — and leaves <em>systematic</em> risk untouched: the market-wide swings every
  diversified portfolio still owns. That residue is exactly what CAPM prices; "fully
  diversified" means "exposed only to the market," not "safe".
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Define beta precisely, and state what it is not.</li>
    <li>Fund A returns 10% with 15% volatility; Fund B returns 8% with 9% volatility; the risk-free rate is 3%. Which wins on the Sharpe ratio, and by how much?</li>
    <li>A stock has 30% volatility and correlates 0.7 with a market of 15% volatility. Compute its beta and its CAPM required return at a 4% risk-free rate and 5% premium — and say why its total 30% risk is not the price.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Beta is covariance with the market divided by market
    variance — the co-movement component only. It is not the asset's total volatility: a
    low-volatility asset can carry high beta and vice versa, and confusing the two misprices
    the discount rate first. (2) Fund A: (10 − 3)/15 ≈ 0.47; Fund B: (8 − 3)/9 ≈ 0.56 — Fund B
    wins, delivering more excess return per unit of total risk, which is exactly the common
    currency the Sharpe ratio exists to provide. (3) Beta = ρ × (σᵢ/σₘ) = 0.7 × (30/15) = 1.4;
    CAPM demands 4 + 1.4 × 5 = 11%. The total 30% volatility is not priced because most of it
    is idiosyncratic — diversifiable at no cost — so only the market-linked slice earns a
    premium; paying for the rest would reward risk investors could have shed for free.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Arithmetic means are the input for expected returns; geometric means describe realised growth — and volatile assets compound worse than their averages suggest.</li>
    <li>Beta is covariance with the market divided by market variance: the market-linked slice of risk, not total volatility.</li>
    <li>Diversification delivers less-than-weighted-average risk; the correlation between assets is the engine of the whole arithmetic.</li>
    <li>CAPM prices one thing: expected return = risk-free rate + beta × market premium; zero-beta earns the risk-free rate, negative-beta earns less.</li>
    <li>Risk is multidimensional — Sortino ratios, maximum drawdown and Value-at-Risk expose what variance hides.</li>
    <li>The security market line comes out flat: low beta earns too much, high beta too little, and size, value and momentum join the toolkit.</li>
    <li>CAPM survives because practice needs a transparent number — cost of equity, hurdle rates, alpha — and every alternative inherits its problems plus new ones.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling expert lesson <em>Behavioural Finance</em> takes the anomalies this page had to
    park — overreaction, loss aversion, the equity premium puzzle — and asks whether they are
    risk factors or systematic errors, which is the field's fork in the road. Further along the
    ladder, <em>Options and Derivatives</em> applies pricing to contingent claims, where
    no-arbitrage replaces the equilibrium argument CAPM rests on.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Why CAPM survives its own funerals.</strong> The model has been declared dead
    routinely since the 1970s (Roll's 1977 critique alone — that the "market portfolio" is
    unobservable, so the model is untestable — should have ended it). It survives because
    every alternative inherits its problems plus new ones: multi-factor models need to pick
    factors (the zoo problem), consumption-based models fail the premium puzzle, and
    practitioners need one number today. The mature stance treats CAPM as a first-order
    approximation whose failure modes are known and nameable: it misprices by ignoring
    size, value, profitability, momentum and investor leverage constraints — and each named
    failure is itself a research programme.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Factor models beyond CAPM.</strong> The Arbitrage Pricing Theory (Ross, 1976)
    generalised pricing: expected return is a linear function of exposures to whatever priced
    factors exist, with arbitrage enforcing the relation rather than equilibrium assumptions.
    Merton's ICAPM (1973) interpreted factors as hedgeable states of the world. The
    practitioner's synthesis is the Fama–French five-factor model (2015: market, size, value,
    profitability, investment) — used exactly as CAPM is, with more columns: a portfolio's
    expected return is its factor exposures priced at factor premia. The deep question — what
    are factors economically? risk, or systematic mispricing — remains the field's fork in the
    road.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The equity premium puzzle.</strong> Historical equity outperformance (roughly 5–6%
    a year over bills, geometric) is too large for plausible risk aversion — Mehra and Prescott
    (1985) showed a rational investor would need implausible risk aversion to hold the observed
    bond-equity mix. Candidate answers: rare disasters (Barro), myopic loss aversion
    (Benartzi–Thaler — investors check too often, and losses hurt about twice as much), and
    habit formation. The puzzle matters practically: the discount rate you choose embeds a
    position on it, and pension accounting worldwide quietly depends on the answer.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Do published anomalies survive publication? (The evidence:
    returns shrink post-publication by about a third — consistent with arbitrage, also
    consistent with data mining.) Does the low-volatility anomaly persist after leverage
    constraints and fees? And can machine-learned factors avoid rediscovering the factor zoo's
    statistical artefacts — the current replication debate in miniature.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Sharpe (1964); Fama & French, JFE (1993); Harvey, Liu & Zhu, RFS (2016); Cochrane, "The Factor Zoo". Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
